<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<assembly manifestVersion="1.0" xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v1">

  <!--
    Deliberately minimal. The .NET SDK and Avalonia already merge in their own compatibility and
    DPI-awareness sections; declaring either of those a second time here makes Windows reject the
    whole manifest with a side-by-side configuration error and the app refuses to launch. The only
    thing this file needs to state is the privilege level.
  -->
  <trustInfo xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v2">
    <security>
      <requestedPrivileges xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v3">
        <!--
          Runs with the user's own rights.

          2.x asked for requireAdministrator, which meant a UAC prompt on every single launch. It
          was only ever needed for one case: typing into a window that is itself running as
          administrator, because Windows refuses synthetic input from a lower integrity level.
          Everything people actually dictate into (browsers, editors, chat, Office) runs
          unelevated, so the prompt was a daily cost for a rare capability.

          It is also meaningless on the other two platforms: Linux and macOS gate this through
          permissions rather than administrator rights, and requesting root there would be wrong.

          Anyone who does need to dictate into an elevated window can start Cloudict as
          administrator; the diagnose command reports whether the session is elevated.
        -->
        <requestedExecutionLevel level="asInvoker" uiAccess="false"/>
      </requestedPrivileges>
    </security>
  </trustInfo>

</assembly>
